  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-x}, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 1-2x, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$",
  "worked_solution": "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1) & =-\\sqrt{-(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}h(x) & =-\\sqrt{-(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=-1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}h(x) & =1-2(-1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
